WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:06.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  pokeymon has always been a series about  going on an adventure discovering new  locations catching and raising pokemon  and becoming the best there ever was  this has held true for every generation  of games the latest entry is Pokemon Sun  and Moon present you with a detailed and  beautiful tropical setting to explore  brand new challenges to face and plenty  of new Pokemon - catch and care for it's  a fun charming little adventure but as  much as I was enjoying my time with it  something felt missing its difficulty  ever since X&amp;Y Pokemon has become  ridiculously easy perhaps from a desire  for accessibility for new players and  while there's still plenty of things to  love about these games the lack of  difficulty not only is off-putting for  someone looking for more of a challenge  but it also diminishes the adventurous  spirit that Pokemon is all about one of  the things that made X&amp;Y so darn easy  was the XP share which had been changed  to apply to the whole party so that  everyone gets some experience from the  battle the result was that I never  needed to grind and my Pokemon were over  leveled for pretty much every situation  Sun and Moon has actually attempted to  course-correct this by changing how much  XP is gained based on the difference  between the levels of you and your  opponent but it doesn't feel fully there  yet  I still rarely enter a battle worried  about the level of my Pokemon or if I  was prepared for the fight aside from  the lack of mental challenge this  created an effect where I suddenly had  far less investment in my pokemons  spending time in the tall grass grinding  with a specific Pokemon so that it  learns that one move I need helped form  an attachment with it but that no longer  happens when I never need to spend any  time grinding anymore and because my  whole party is getting XP from battles  there was no reason to switch them  around as often meaning I have a  situation where I only really use two or  three members of my party and the rest  are faceless pokemons that I have no  attachment to and can be swapped out  without a second thought these games  have really liked to put an emphasis on  the friendships between you and your  pokemons but they significantly reduce  the need to interact with them directly  another dynamic that has changed is your  rival a Pokemon staple you are always  given an opposing character who  counter pick whichever started Pokemon  you pick so if you pick Charmander and  pokemons read your rival will pick  squirtle those moments where your rival  would show up out of nowhere felt tense  because they always had the advantage  and you've had a stake and not wanting  to lose to them because they were such a  real jerk to you but ever since  generation 3 your rival has been your  character's friend with a healthy  competition towards each other this  removes a lot of attention towards them  and gives me much less of a reason to  care when they want to fight this  reaches its peak in Sun and Moon where  your rival actually picks the weaker  pokemons so battles against them are a  total breeze I never felt any stress  when he randomly showed up and his  attitude towards everything is so  carefree that referring to him as a  rival it doesn't even make sense anymore  well the pokemons themselves have always  been the main focus I think something  often overlooked in these games are the  environments themselves and the  interesting challenges they can present  because Pokemon centers were always  located only in towns and cities there  was a real sense of danger and risk that  came from leaving each town and  exploring the many routes forests and  caves throughout the world unaware of  how long it would be until the next time  you could heal and the newer games are  so many Pokemon Center is littered about  and NPCs who will just straight-up heal  you to the point where I never felt  concerned about being low on health as I  was exploring a route in Sun and Moon  just when I thought to myself I should  head back to town and heal before moving  on the NPC next to me graciously offered  to heal my Pokemon whenever I wanted  instead of that original Pokemon feeling  of being a kid going on a dangerous but  rewarding adventure where I have to  prove myself I'm instead on an easy  journey where nothing could go wrong  thanks to the many adults and safety  nets nearby and this feeling is cemented  with Sun and moons removal of the  dungeon like caves the caves and the  older games always felt like huge  milestones in your journey you had to  conquer there are multi-level labyrinths  that were easy to get lost in and would  feature puzzles such as blocks you had  to push out of the way or through holes  so they could drop to a different floor  and ice puzzles that you needed to find  the correct path through the caves  always felt like the areas where the  game wasn't afraid to beat on the player  a bit like the infamous Rock Tunnel from  red and blue where you had to stumble  through blind unless your Pokemon knew  flash with each cave I'd find myself  preparing by getting my pokemon healed  up  pokeballs potions and good old escape  ropes before diving in exploring every  nook and cranny led to items and even  rare Pokemon and when you finally found  the exit you were rewarded with a huge  sense of relief and satisfaction as you  saw the next town and its Pokemon Center  compare this to Sun and Moon where the  first cave I came across was a simple  single leveled environment with barely  any side paths and no puzzles to speak  of and before I knew it I had already  found the exit I'm surprised the game  still has the escape rope as an item  because the player is never in a  situation that demands a quick getaway  perhaps the most insulting instance is  when I had to travel to the top of a  volcano for the next trial so I go to  the base of the mountain go through some  grass fight a single trainer and then I  come across the cave entrance but rather  than leading to a cave proper it  instantly teleports me to the top where  a person congratulate Smee on making it  this high and then heals my Pokemon in  an interaction that I'm pretty sure is  supposed to be genuine but came off as  incredibly pandering this was a prime  opportunity to make me work for my trial  with a memorable trek up this volcano  but instead it skips all that and  rewards me like I accomplished some huge  feat that I never actually felt like I  did at another example of this kind of  more guided experience in the series is  the recent treatment of the mascot  legendary Pokemon which are the ones on  the boxes with each generation these  legend areas have become more important  to the game's overall narrative  which means you're not only guaranteed  to come across them but are required to  catch them for the story to move forward  ever since black and white they have  jacked up the catch rate of the mascot  legendaries making them ridiculously  easy to catch not that it matters if you  do fail because you were given as many  chances as it takes until you catch them  compare that to Pokemon silverware if  I've wanted to catch lugia I needed to  make my way to the world through Islands  traverse a complex cave to find them and  then actually catch the darn thing which  could take dozens of attempts I remember  spending what felt like hours reloading  my save whenever I ran out of pokeballs  are accidentally knocked them out which  may sound terrible but the satisfaction  of hearing that click when I finally  caught them was so rewarding and that  feeling just isn't possible when there  are no stakes to it the reason games  like Sun and Moon still have Legendary's  and rare Pokemon that demand players  devote time and energy to finding them  the  are all post credit activities and it's  in some of these side activities where  pokemons retained some of its challenge  things like the Battle Royale which  present a 4-way match up where you need  to think more carefully about what moves  you use and who to attack and those who  choose to enter the realm of battles  with real players will discover lots of  hidden depth that the battle system  still has to offer and I think Sun and  Moon is not quite the cakewalk that xny  was the totem Pokemon battles for  example can be tricky with beefed up  stats and pokemons allies that show up  but I still maintain the mainline  experience that Pokemon presents is far  too easy and lessens the overall  memorability of your pokemons journey  and at least to me that's kind of the  whole point of Pokemon I don't need  these games to be some hardcore brutal  experience but I expected a rewarding  journey that really makes me connect  with my Pokemon and the world around me  but there needs to be more weight and  challenge for that to happen otherwise  victory road doesn't really feel like  much of a victory anymore  [Music]

